// A library that ships a specification of its own: the external `.wasm` behind
// `spec_adopted_extern.inf` and `spec_adopted_both.inf`, compiled on its own in
// proof mode so it carries the `inference.spec_funcs` and `inference.hspecs`
// sections recording what its author proved about it. A library compiled for
// execution carries neither, so only a proof-mode one has anything to adopt.
//
// `ScaleSpec` is universal, which is the only kind a link can adopt: an
// `exists` or `unique` judgment reduces its own specification function's body,
// and a specification function is outside every export closure and so never
// crosses the merge.
//
// The obligation applies `scale`, the one function a program importing this
// library pulls across the merge, so an adopted copy of it names a body the
// merged module actually contains.
//
// The scale factor is a five-digit prime rather than a round number because the
// end-to-end tests identify the merged body by it: they read the ordinal the
// adopted obligation applies out of the emitted proof and then confirm the body
// at that ordinal is this one, by a literal no other body in the artifact
// carries. A `Definition` name cannot serve, since the translator sanitizes the
// merged symbol's separators away.
//
// No data segment: the merged module built from this one is compiled by `coqc`,
// and the corpus rejects a data segment in any module it gates — reported
// against the main fixture's name rather than this one.

pub fn scale(a: i32) -> i32 {
  return a * 10007;
}

spec ScaleSpec {
  fn scales() forall {
    let x: i32 = @;
    assert(scale(x) == x * 10007);
  }
}
